---
title: Combining two maps
---
Two maps, each from a pipeline of its own, joined into one: the talk’s point that a map rarely needs just one technique [[t:179-185 s:20]].

## The rule
1. Build the first input on a map of its own. The demo’s: rooms in subdivided space [[c09:41-79]].
2. Build the second input on a map of its own. The demo’s: cellular automata [[c09:82-114]].
3. Copy the left half of the result from the first map and the right half from the second, column for column. Nothing joins them [[c09:100-114]].

- **Talk’s example:** a fortress on the left that breaks out into an underworld of caves on the right [[t:181-185]].
- **Then a prefab:** the talk’s author adds a pre-designed section in the middle: fortifications [[t:186-188]].

